menu "Dancefloor LEDs"

    config DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        bool "Enable LED visualiser (FFT + beat detection + strip)"
        default y
        help
            Turn off to test whether the FFT and LED refresh are stealing time
            from audio -- it is the heaviest thing sharing core 1 with playback.

    config DANCEFLOOR_LED_GPIO
        int "WS2812 data GPIO"
        default 18
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        help
            Driven as SPI2 MOSI through the GPIO matrix. Needs a 74AHCT125 level
            shifter: 3.3 V data into a 5 V WS2812 is unreliable without one.

    config DANCEFLOOR_LED_COUNT
        int "Number of LEDs"
        default 8
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        help
            Total pixels on the one data line, so chained 8-LED sticks add up:
            four of them is 32, not 8.

            8 is the bring-up figure and small enough to run off the devkit's
            5 V rail. The real strips are 1-2 m and need their own supply.

    choice DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE
        prompt "Strip model"
        default DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE_WS2812
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        help
            Wire order differs between models. Getting this wrong swaps colours
            rather than failing, so it looks like a bug in the patterns.

        config DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE_WS2812
            bool "WS2812 / WS2812B (5 V, GRB)"

        config DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE_WS2811
            bool "WS2811 (12 V, BRG as wired here, 1 IC per 3 LEDs)"

        config DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE_SK6812
            bool "SK6812 RGBW (5 V, GRBW)"
    endchoice

    config DANCEFLOOR_LED_BRIGHTNESS
        int "Brightness cap (percent)"
        default 10
        range 1 100
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        help
            Scales every pixel before it is sent. LEDs are the largest single
            load in the power budget -- ~150 pixels at full white is ~45 W --
            and eyes are dark-adapted outdoors at night, so a low figure looks
            no dimmer than a high one and reclaims most of that.

            40 was the original design figure and was too bright on the bench.
            Below about 10 the 8-bit colour resolution starts to show: the
            brightest component becomes 25, so hue steps get visibly coarse and
            the dim tail of the beat envelope quantises to black.

    config DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_LED_MARKER
        bool "LED sync marker (bench instrument)"
        default n
        help
            Flashes once per second of master-clock time, on every unit at the
            same instant. Put two boards side by side: if the onboard LEDs blink
            together the whole chain agrees, and if one lags it is obvious with
            no console and no wiring.

            It shows something nothing else does. Every existing measurement
            covers the AUDIO path -- the audio marker, AUDIO SYNC, TRACK
            DIVERGENCE. Between playback and the pixels sit the analysis stream
            buffer, the FFT, the detector and the render, and none of that is
            measured anywhere. The strips could be visibly out of step with the
            speakers perfectly aligned and nothing would report it.

            The second boundary comes from due_us, which is derived from the
            scheduled instant the audio carries, so every unit crosses it on the
            same block with nothing sent between them.

            Same discipline as the audio marker: nothing corrects on it, so this
            changes no behaviour. It costs one GPIO write per analysis block.

    config DANCEFLOOR_LED_MARKER_GPIO
        int "GPIO for the LED sync marker"
        default 2
        range 0 33
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_LED_MARKER
        help
            Which pin to flash. 2 is the default because it is the onboard LED
            on the most common ESP32 boards, but this varies and there is no way
            to detect it -- so if the LED stays dark while the log says the
            marker fired, this is the setting to change.

            Boards seen in the wild:

              GPIO 2   DOIT ESP32 DEVKIT V1, NodeMCU-32S, most WROOM-32 clones
              GPIO 5   WEMOS/LOLIN D32, some TTGO
              GPIO 16  a few ESP32-CAM style boards
              GPIO 22  some Heltec boards -- but 22 is the SBC UART TX on the
                       hub, so only usable on a satellite
              none     official Espressif ESP32-DevKitC has only a power LED

            With no usable onboard LED, wire one to any free pin through a
            resistor, or point this at a pin you can watch on a scope. Taken
            here: I2S (25, 26, 27), the strip (18), the markers (4, 21), and on
            the hub the SBC UART (22, 23).

            The range stops at 33 because GPIO 34-39 on the ESP32 are
            input-only and cannot drive anything.

            The eye resolves maybe 10-20 ms and a 240 fps phone camera about
            4 ms, so this answers "are they together" rather than "by how much"
            -- the numbers are in AUDIO SYNC and TRACK DIVERGENCE.

    config DANCEFLOOR_LED_PATTERN
        string "Pattern to start with"
        default "pulse"
        help
            Which pattern the strip runs from boot. Must match a name from
            pattern_by_name() -- run `tools/pattern_lab --list` to see them.
            An unknown name logs a warning and leaves the first pattern
            running, so a typo costs a line in the log rather than a dark floor.

            "pulse" follows onsets across all four bands, which suits music
            whose transients are broadband.

            "boom" follows the lowest band alone, 43-129 Hz, and is meant for
            forró: the mallet stroke on the zabumba's big head, with the
            triangle and the accordion deliberately not wanted. Try both against
            a recording in pattern_lab before deciding -- where the two disagree
            on the same track, one of them is wrong about the music, and which
            one depends entirely on what is playing.

endmenu
